You are working on Leaflet, an open-source JavaScript library for mobile-friendly interactive maps.

# Code Style
- Use tabs for indentation, spaces for alignment.
- Use ES module syntax (import/export) with .js file extensions in import paths.
- Always use curly braces for control structures.
- Comments must have a space after // or /*.
- Prefer ** over Math.pow() and Object.hasOwn() over hasOwnProperty.
- Do not leave unused variables. Caught errors in catch blocks are exempt.

# Project Structure
- src/ contains the library source, organised into core/, dom/, geo/, geometry/, layer/, map/, control/.
- spec/suites/ contains Mocha/Chai tests run via Karma.
- dist/ contains generated build output - never edit directly.
- build/ contains Rollup config and doc generation scripts.
- docs/ contains a Jekyll-based documentation site.

# Patterns
- Classes use Leaflet's custom OOP system: Class.extend(), Class.include(), Class.mergeOptions().
- Events use the Evented mixin: on(), off(), fire(), listens().
- Layer options are declared in a static options object and merged via L.Util.setOptions().
- DOM manipulation should go through src/dom/ utilities.

# Commands
- Install: npm install
- Build: npm run build
- Lint: npm run lint
- Fix lint: npm run lintfix
- Test: npm test -- --single-run
- Watch: npm run watch

# Guidelines
- Keep changes small and focused; Leaflet prioritises simplicity and small bundle size.
- Ensure broad browser compatibility; follow baseline-js/use-baseline ESLint rules.
- Build the project before running tests.
- API docs are generated from Leafdoc comments in source files.
- Do not commit to main directly; use topic branches.
